WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.469
John Cusack has played some of the most iconic
roles of all time.

00:00:03.469 --> 00:00:08.810
From Say Anything to High Fidelity, Bullets
Over Broadway to Being John Malkovich, Cusack

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:10.970
has charmed audiences for decades.

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:15.321
But although the talented, versatile actor
has been in the business over 30 years, his

00:00:15.321 --> 00:00:17.610
career isn't what it used to be.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:18.610
Why?

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:23.130
Let's take a look back and see why John Cusack
hasn't graced our screens for a while.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.550
He called Hollywood "a whorehouse"

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:30.120
In an interview with The Guardian about his
role in the Hollywood satire Map to the Stars,

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.880
John Cusack came clean about his views on
the strange world of Hollywood.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:38.710
Cusack commented that the old ways of filmmaking
are gone and now everything revolves around

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:43.079
franchises and huge stars...which isn't really
all that off the mark.

00:00:43.079 --> 00:00:47.320
As he puts it, "The culture just eats young
actors up and spits them out.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.149
It's a hard thing to survive without finding
safe harbor."

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:54.680
It's easy to understand his seeming hesitation
to fight for stardom in an industry he's lost

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.100
trust in considering his views on Hollywood.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:02.070
He went on to describe it by saying, "[Hollywood
is] a whorehouse and people go mad."

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:03.719
Writing films

00:01:03.719 --> 00:01:08.979
Not content to be a just an actor, Cusack
has co-written a few of his most popular films.

00:01:08.979 --> 00:01:13.639
He co-wrote the screenplays for High Fidelity
and Grosse Pointe Blank, proving that he knows

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.340
a few things about dark comedy and oddball
characters.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.600
"Did I listen to pop music because I was miserable?

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:22.920
Or was I miserable because I listened to pop
music?"

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:29.880
More recently, he co-wrote the political film
War, Inc. as well as We Are Not Animals, which

00:01:29.899 --> 00:01:33.189
is, fittingly, a film about an actor getting
tired of Hollywood.

00:01:33.189 --> 00:01:36.409
Though it doesn't look like he has any new
screenplays in the works as of the making

00:01:36.409 --> 00:01:39.380
of this video, he's still been writing quite
a bit.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:43.960
The only difference is he's turned his writing
efforts to the political arena.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:45.469
Politically active

00:01:45.469 --> 00:01:49.179
John Cusack isn't your typical actor interested
in the political world.

00:01:49.179 --> 00:01:53.899
When Visa, Mastercard, and Paypal all stopped
taking payments intended for Wikileaks in

00:01:53.899 --> 00:01:59.219
2010, Cusack became a founding member of the
Freedom of the Press Foundation in response.

00:01:59.219 --> 00:02:03.299
The Foundation's main goal is to protect the
privacy of the journalists willing to deal

00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:05.270
with potentially dangerous subjects.

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:12.400
"It's an advocacy group just uh, try to protect
the rights of journalists to do their job.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:13.439
Protect their sources."

00:02:13.439 --> 00:02:17.870
Through crowdfunding, they help different
press organizations stay afloat regardless

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:19.120
of outside pressures.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.180
Cusack has written many articles for the site
and is clearly passionate about the issue

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:24.310
of freedom of speech.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:29.840
Cusack has also written over 20 articles for
the Huffington Post, and in 2014, he traveled

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.430
to Russia with Arundhati Roy and Daniel Ellsberg
to meet with Edward Snowden.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:38.709
Roy and Cusack wrote a series of essays about
their conversations with Snowden which they

00:02:38.709 --> 00:02:41.599
formed into the book Things That Can and Cannot
Be Said.

00:02:41.599 --> 00:02:45.959
Cusack is still devoted to activism and it's
obvious he's not happy just resting on his

00:02:45.959 --> 00:02:48.450
celebrity — he wants to make a real difference.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:52.630
And with so much time spent in the political
arena, it's no wonder he doesn't light up

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:54.530
the marquee as much anymore.

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:56.489
Aging out of the rom-com

00:02:56.489 --> 00:03:01.030
Cusack started out as a teenage heartthrob,
playing the sweet, slightly nerdy guy who'd

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:02.410
do anything for his girl.

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:04.879
"Gee, I'm real sorry your mom blew up Ricky."

00:03:04.879 --> 00:03:07.069
But nobody can play the handsome leading man
forever.

00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:11.650
Though Cusack often branched out to do less
mainstream work like Being John Malkovich

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:15.549
and Midnight in the Garden of Good and Evil,
he kept taking lead roles.

00:03:15.549 --> 00:03:19.870
And those movies got worse and worse — at
least according to critics...and box office

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:20.870
receipts.

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:26.030
Sure, he's got High Fidelity, but he also
made Must Love Dogs, Serendipity, and America's

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:29.620
Sweethearts, and you didn't even realize those
clips were out of order.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:31.730
And he definitely wasn't in American Beauty.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:32.730
"American Beauty?

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.700
What's funny about that?"

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:36.300
"You were in that"

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:37.800
"No I wasn't!"

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:38.580
American Beauty?"

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:43.150
It seems that mainstream Hollywood wants to
put him in leading roles in dull action flicks

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:47.450
or rom-coms, whereas Cusack clearly wants
to go in a different direction, like the killer

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:50.430
roles he played in The Frozen Ground and The
Paperboy.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:53.980
When Hollywood only wants to see you play
the same thing over and over again, it's no

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:58.739
wonder Cusack is a little hesitant to keep
taking on huge leads.

00:03:58.739 --> 00:04:00.129
Straight to DVD

00:04:00.129 --> 00:04:03.040
Cusack hasn't been in as many blockbusters
as he used to.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:05.049
But that doesn't mean he's given up acting.

00:04:05.049 --> 00:04:07.730
In fact, Cusack's been making tons of movies.

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:12.079
Between 2012 and 2016, he made a whopping
17 films.

00:04:12.079 --> 00:04:16.239
The reason you might not be aware of that
insane productivity is that most of the films

00:04:16.239 --> 00:04:19.519
wind up going straight to video-on-demand
or DVD.

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:24.780
And they tend to be...well, unimpressive,
like Reclaim or the fake-sounding Drive Hard.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:30.530
Four of his films since 2014 got critics'
ratings below 5% on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:32.729
That's full-blown Nicolas Cage territory.

00:04:32.729 --> 00:04:36.210
"I'm gonna try and kill the man!"

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:37.210
Just kidding.

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:40.229
Nic Cage is in a league of his own.

00:04:40.229 --> 00:04:41.729
Critically ignored

00:04:41.729 --> 00:04:44.819
That's not to say that John Cusack's recent
movies are all bad.

00:04:44.819 --> 00:04:49.419
In 2015, he starred in Love &amp; Mercy as an
older Brian Wilson — frontman for The Beach

00:04:49.419 --> 00:04:52.770
Boys — dealing with mental illness and a
controlling manager.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:57.400
The film received excellent reviews, with
many praising Cusack's fine work in particular.

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:02.310
But the film only made $12 million domestically,
and though there was some speculation about

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:05.440
a potential Oscar nomination, Cusack came
up short.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:10.360
He wasn't nominated for a single award the
entire season, and the film itself was similarly

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:12.150
shut out.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:13.500
Things to come

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:17.270
Though it may seem like Cusack's star has
dimmed, all it's really done is split into

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:19.229
a constellation of different projects.

00:05:19.229 --> 00:05:22.259
John Cusack won't be fading into the background
any time soon.

00:05:22.259 --> 00:05:27.080
His political writing and activism will surely
keep him busy in the next few years, and he's

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:28.840
already got another film lined up.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:32.910
With more than 30 years in the acting business,
we're pretty sure he's got more than a few

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:34.930
more iconic parts up his sleeve.

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:35.979
Thanks for watching!

00:05:35.979 --> 00:05:39.150
